  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Determining the Overlap Interval",
    "lesson_core_move": "Intersect the support of $x(\\tau)$ with the support of $h(t-\\tau)$ for a fixed $t$ to determine where the convolution integrand is nonzero.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Determining the Overlap Interval",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Determining the Overlap Interval",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the previous lessons, you found the support of $x(\\tau)$ and the support of $h(t-\\tau)$ separately. The next move is to combine those two support intervals in the convolution variable $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The product $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$ can be nonzero only at values of $\\tau$ where both factors are nonzero at the same time. That common set of $\\tau$ values is called the overlap interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau) \\ne 0 \\quad \\text{only where} \\quad \\tau \\in \\operatorname{supp} x(\\tau) \\cap \\operatorname{supp} h(t-\\tau).",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For example, suppose $x(\\tau)$ is nonzero on $0\\le \\tau\\le 2$, and $h(t-\\tau)$ is nonzero on $t-3\\le \\tau\\le t-1$. While finding overlap, treat $t$ as fixed and decide which $\\tau$ values satisfy both interval conditions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In general, the left endpoint of the overlap comes from the larger lower endpoint, and the right endpoint comes from the smaller upper endpoint.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{If the supports are } [a,b] \\text{ and } [c(t),d(t)], \\text{ then overlap is } [\\max(a,c(t)),\\min(b,d(t))].",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Later sections will compare these endpoints to find the actual overlap interval in each time region. For now, the key rule is: overlap means the intersection of the fixed support of $x(\\tau)$ and the moving support of $h(t-\\tau)$, with $t$ held fixed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Determining the Overlap Interval",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Intersecting Supports for One Fixed $t$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Suppose $x(\\tau)$ is nonzero on $0 \\le \\tau \\le 4$ and $h(t-\\tau)$ is nonzero on $t-3 \\le \\tau \\le t-1$. Determine the overlap interval when $t=5$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the fixed value $t=5$. First substitute that value into the moving support interval for $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-3 \\le \\tau \\le t-1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 5-3 \\le \\tau \\le 5-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2 \\le \\tau \\le 4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compare this moving support with the fixed support of $x(\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{supp} x(\\tau): 0 \\le \\tau \\le 4, \\qquad \\operatorname{supp} h(5-\\tau): 2 \\le \\tau \\le 4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The overlap interval must satisfy both support conditions. Use the larger lower endpoint and the smaller upper endpoint.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\max(0,2) \\le \\tau \\le \\min(4,4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2 \\le \\tau \\le 4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the lower endpoint $2$ is not greater than the upper endpoint $4$, the interval is nonempty. The overlap interval when $t=5$ is $2 \\le \\tau \\le 4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Determining the Overlap Interval",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Writing Overlap with Lower and Upper Endpoint Rules",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Suppose $x(\\tau)$ is nonzero on $0 \\le \\tau \\le 4$ and $h(t-\\tau)$ is nonzero on $t-3 \\le \\tau \\le t-1$. Write the overlap interval using lower- and upper-endpoint rules.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that no numerical value of $t$ is given. Instead of substituting for $t$, keep the endpoints symbolic and use the endpoint rule for intersecting intervals.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{supp} x(\\tau): 0 \\le \\tau \\le 4, \\qquad \\operatorname{supp} h(t-\\tau): t-3 \\le \\tau \\le t-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The lower endpoint of the overlap must be at least as large as both lower endpoints. The two lower endpoints are $0$ and $t-3$, so the overlap starts at their maximum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L(t)=\\max(0,t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The upper endpoint of the overlap must be no larger than both upper endpoints. The two upper endpoints are $4$ and $t-1$, so the overlap ends at their minimum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "U(t)=\\min(4,t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the symbolic overlap interval is written by placing the maximum lower endpoint on the left and the minimum upper endpoint on the right.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\max(0,t-3) \\le \\tau \\le \\min(4,t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This endpoint-rule interval applies on values of $t$ where the lower endpoint is not greater than the upper endpoint. Do not split it into cases yet; the goal here is only to record the overlap in max/min form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Determining the Overlap Interval",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding the $t$ Values Where Endpoint Order Changes",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the endpoint-rule overlap $\\max(0,t-3) \\le \\tau \\le \\min(4,t-1)$, find the $t$ values where the lower endpoint choice or upper endpoint choice can change.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the endpoint-rule form. A chosen endpoint can change only when the two expressions competing inside the same $\\max$ or $\\min$ become equal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\max(0,t-3) \\le \\tau \\le \\min(4,t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the lower endpoint, the competing expressions inside the maximum are $0$ and $t-3$. Set them equal to find where the lower endpoint choice can switch.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0=t-3 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad t=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the upper endpoint, the competing expressions inside the minimum are $4$ and $t-1$. Set them equal to find where the upper endpoint choice can switch.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4=t-1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad t=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "List the switch values in increasing order. These are only the possible endpoint-change values; they are not yet a full piecewise overlap description.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{critical } t\\text{-values: } 3,\\ 5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Determining the Overlap Interval",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Determining Overlap Intervals Across Time Regions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the active overlap range $1 \\le t \\le 7$, suppose the endpoint-rule overlap is $\\max(0,t-3) \\le \\tau \\le \\min(4,t-1)$. Determine the overlap interval in each time region where the endpoint formulas differ.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the active overlap range together with the endpoint-rule expression. The endpoint choices can change at $t=3$ and $t=5$, so use those values to split the active range.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "1 \\le t \\le 3, \\qquad 3 \\le t \\le 5, \\qquad 5 \\le t \\le 7",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On the first region, $1\\le t\\le 3$. The larger lower endpoint is $0$ because $t-3\\le 0$, and the smaller upper endpoint is $t-1$ because $t-1\\le 4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "1\\le t\\le 3: \\qquad 0 \\le \\tau \\le t-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On the middle region, $3\\le t\\le 5$. Now $t-3$ is the larger lower endpoint, while $t-1$ is still the smaller upper endpoint.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3\\le t\\le 5: \\qquad t-3 \\le \\tau \\le t-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On the final region, $5\\le t\\le 7$. The lower endpoint remains $t-3$, but the smaller upper endpoint is now $4$ because $4\\le t-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5\\le t\\le 7: \\qquad t-3 \\le \\tau \\le 4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "These intervals describe where $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$ can be nonzero in each time region. They are overlap intervals only; setting integral bounds and evaluating the convolution come later.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
